  "tour": {
    "title": "Genesis 6:1–4: Sons of God, Nephilim, and the Flood",
    "intro": "This tour starts with what Genesis actually says, then lets you hear the main interpretations—including where later Jewish tradition goes beyond the four verses. From there it follows the passage’s literary links to Genesis 3 and its purpose as the final escalation of violence and rebellion before the flood.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:genesis-1-11-q-r",
        "startSec": 2160,
        "endSec": 2340,
        "why": "Begin here for a careful orientation to the strange passage itself. The teachers distinguish the text from later elaboration: “The half-breeds. Well, it doesn’t say that,” and explain how the Book of Enoch developed the angelic-offspring-and-giants reading while Genesis itself calls the Nephilim mighty warriors and men of renown.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 1-11 Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e1d03584-54ec-43f1-a199-59f940b9f1b5/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e1d03584-54ec-43f1-a199-59f940b9f1b5",
        "durationSec": 2761,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:417c1f86-com-2f63c731-32e0-3416-a86c-8ce581c0de7f",
        "startSec": 1150,
        "endSec": 1590,
        "why": "This gives you the interpretive map before settling on an answer: spiritual beings, powerful human rulers, or the godly line of Seth. It also emphasizes a key textual caution—that the Nephilim are not explicitly said to be the offspring—and then shows why the paragraph is positioned before the flood: it portrays the spread of sin, human depravity, and God’s resolve to “bring things back into line.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 4:1-6:8",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/ba4rg9y6b4ksgv6v/genesis_41-6889j29.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1767,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:gods-spirit-flood-narrative",
        "startSec": 640,
        "endSec": 1130,
        "why": "Now listen for the passage’s literary design. The teachers identify “sons of Elohim” as heavenly-court beings, connect “saw…good…and took” to Genesis 3, and describe Genesis 6 as an inverted fall narrative. They also place the Nephilim in the ancient world’s warrior-king imagery, while noting that the word’s primary sense may be “fallen in battle,” not automatically fallen angels.",
        "episodeTitle": "God’s Spirit in the Flood Narrative",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/6fed2ba8-01ae-4fdb-8afe-9c7ab3232f32/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=6fed2ba8-01ae-4fdb-8afe-9c7ab3232f32",
        "durationSec": 3585,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:edf9e365-6859-4f4d-947e-9cfc491ea29b",
        "startSec": 410,
        "endSec": 590,
        "why": "End with the clearest answer to why these verses appear immediately before the flood. The teachers say the point is not to satisfy curiosity about “demonic human babies,” but to show that rebellion is spiraling out of control: humanity’s mandate to “be fruitful and multiply” is being reversed, with a broken image spreading through the world.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 6-11: The Flood",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG5000636430.mp3?updated=1664486134",
        "durationSec": 3274,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips leave you with both humility and clarity: Genesis 6:1–4 is deliberately strange, and interpreters have disagreed about the identity of the sons of God. But the passage’s narrative role is much clearer. It shows boundary-breaking power, violent renown, and creation’s corruption reaching a crisis point—setting the stage for the flood as judgment and reset.",
    "totalSeconds": 1290
  },
